there's been a long love-hate  relationship with cities that big and  messy in new york city or in shanghai  it's easier to go up 300 meters than it  is to travel horizontally 300 meters  during much of the day if we don't find  the solutions in cities then we're going  to have a hard time finding the  solutions because that's where most of  people are living and that is going to  increasingly be the case cities emerged  about 10,000 years ago the conventional  wisdom is that when we move to settled  agriculture 10,000 years or so guard  cities or towns that were began to grow  as agglomerations of population to do  with the division of labor the  increasing surplus in society and so on  and you know for for thousands of years  technological constraints limited the  size of cities humans since ever in all  settings travel about 70 minutes per day  whether it's rice farmers going to their  fields students going to elementary  schools or commuters in fact the best  definition of a city i think is the area  that you can reach in 35 minutes and  then get back home again that is to say  that from the center to the periphery  that that round trip of about 70 minutes  which is about what people are willing  to travel each day really defines an  urban area we're crossing the fifty  percent of the world population living  in cities now that is in the developed  world already up to eighty eighty-five  percent and since the developed world  countries are creating many of the  environmental problems that we're seeing  globally now that level of urbanization  is something to really think about in  terms of how do we have to modify our  theory in order to take into account the  prevalence and predominance of this one  species human species that inhabit  certain ecosystems  china is a living laboratory of rapid  urban growth the world's most populous  country got a late start on urbanization  but is catching up quickly today around  forty five percent of the population  lives in cities the sheer scale of this  extreme makeover is enormous some eighty  nine cities of million people and seven  city said more than five million people  this rapid urbanization has caused some  significant problems I'm strolling down  a hoot on a rare and vanishing example  of a traditional Beijing neighborhood  people here heat their homes using coal  in fact coal consumption coal combustion  is the predominant form of energy  electricity and hot water heating here  in Beijing today the air is quite  breathable but some days the air reeks  of sulfur and the pea soup is like  London of the early 20th century no  wonder then that Beijing officials worry  about air quality for the upcoming  summer olympics but really it's the most  vulnerable the very young the elderly  who are at risk from this very heavy  pollution burden the environmental  Kuznets curve is very simple initially  richer is dirtier and then later richer  is cleaner when first when people first  start to have higher incomes they chew  through lots of stuff they chop down  lots of trees they burn coal they make a  make a mess but then when they in turn  get a bit richer after that cleanliness  environment safety those values become  more important and they have the  resources to invest so you have a kind  of art where with rising incomes  initially life the economy cities become  dirtier and then later on they become  cleaner and I think what we're seeing in  many societies now is a very very strong  preference for a rising share of  people's wealth and income to be  dedicated to Environmental Quality  the misconception is that there is a  nature out there and then there is a  society over here and so if people are  living in cities they're not living in  nature they're not living in an  ecosystem in an ecological system and in  fact that's not the case urban  environments are ecosystems they have  living and nonliving components that  interact they are areas that in which  ecological processes are going on all  the time we can learn a lot from looking  at urban ecosystems in the same way that  we look at other ecosystems but what we  have learned in years of study of urban  ecosystems is that we can't ignore the  fact that they are dominated by one  species this human species and that we  have to use the tools and approaches  that have been developed in social  sciences to really understand the  interactions between the social system  and the ecological system  if you look at city plans they're still  rather top-down rather idealistic they  don't really relate to what cities are  actually like as it were but of our  scientific viewers begun to change that  it's much more to do with the fact that  this kind of messiness of cities is is  effectively a manifestation of a kind of  underlying order and that we need to  take this into account so that's a  change that's going on at the moment and  in fact if you look at the on my on my  computer screen here I have a picture of  London which is the London the form of  London something like 40 miles across  and 40 miles north it's the actual  picture of the spread of the city and it  looks like a kind of a cancer it looks  messy looks angry and that's been the  predominant view of cities I think from  all sorts of perspectives but and that's  the thing that's begun to change  recently so the digital world is giving  this as this ability to learn more about  cities but at the same time of course we  have to be conscious of the fact that  cities are also responding and changing  to the very existence of this digital  world it's kind of two-way traffic I  mean we get to know more about the city  but the same time cities are changing  with response to these technological  technological developments now in terms  of actually thinking about cities  thinking about how we might go about  communicating these ideas to people then  clearly the idea of representing the  city digitally is important and again  I'm looking at this movie of Canary  Wharf here and you can actually see that  this effect effectively is like a a land  use plan showing you the location of  different land uses in the world but  it's also in 3d and we can manipulate it  we can fly through it because we can put  it on the web we can actually  communicate the plan to a whole range of  people in a very effective way now that  is something which is very new and in a  digital world where we have the ability  to link large numbers of people to this  sort of these sort of visualizations  then that's a very effective  beginning to think about a kind of a  planning of cities that involves a much  wider constituency of people than we've  ever had the ability to do previously  the 20th century experienced in my view  of the great misfortune of the the  development of the suburbs I don't like  the suburbs I regard suburban Knights as  castrated peasants you can't even keep a  chicken in the suburbs for fear that the  neighbors will complain because of the  noise so I hope that we'll see in the  21st century decline in that environment  and again a rise of true and beautiful  cities the American writer Gertrude  Stein in about the year nineteen thirty  remarked that at that time in 1930 the  United States was the oldest country in  the world because the United States had  been in the 20th century longer than any  other country and in 2008 I would say  that cities like Hong Kong and Shanghai  and Bangalore have now been in the 21st  century longer than London or Paris or  New York or Chicago and so I think we  really need to look to these new cities  for experience and models we will cities  be 10 years from now 20 years from now a  century from now expect in the near  future eco-cities to arise these cities  will use renewable resources to try to  reduce their carbon footprint the ratio  of the carbon they emit to the carbon  that's absorbed in the city it's  heartening to imagine that humanity can  summon the courage to radically rethink  our concept of cities and create living  spaces in harmony with nature before  this vision can be fully realized  however we must solve the problems the  many discouraging problems of the urban  lifestyle that three billion of us have  chosen to lead today scientists must  take the lead in helping to draw up the  blueprints for the sustainable cities of  the future in this special issue on  cities find news reviews and  perspectives on urbanization health  reproduction traffic  Shin water energy and much more  you  science is published by triple ass the  science society

